<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sieg (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sieg is a river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 155 km long, rising in the Rothaar Mountains and flowing west through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegerland">Siegerland</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>. In Roman times its valley lay in the land of the Sugambri, and the Siegerland was later an important iron-mining district. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a> takes its name from it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sieg-river.jpg" alt="Sieg (river)" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lencer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Verlaufskarte_Sieg.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
